Task 1: Install and Configure Prometheus
Subtask 1.1: Download and Install Prometheus
First, let's create a dedicated user for Prometheus and download the latest version.

# Create prometheus user
sudo useradd --no-create-home --shell /bin/false prometheus

# Create directories for Prometheus
sudo mkdir /etc/prometheus
sudo mkdir /var/lib/prometheus

# Set ownership
sudo chown prometheus:prometheus /etc/prometheus
sudo chown prometheus:prometheus /var/lib/prometheus

# Download Prometheus (version 2.47.0 - current stable)
cd /tmp
wget https://github.com/prometheus/prometheus/releases/download/v2.47.0/prometheus-2.47.0.linux-amd64.tar.gz

# Extract the archive
tar xvf prometheus-2.47.0.linux-amd64.tar.gz
cd prometheus-2.47.0.linux-amd64
Subtask 1.2: Install Prometheus Binaries
# Copy binaries to system directories
sudo cp prometheus /usr/local/bin/
sudo cp promtool /usr/local/bin/

# Set ownership for binaries
sudo chown prometheus:prometheus /usr/local/bin/prometheus
sudo chown prometheus:prometheus /usr/local/bin/promtool

# Copy configuration files
sudo cp -r consoles /etc/prometheus
sudo cp -r console_libraries /etc/prometheus

# Set ownership for configuration directories
sudo chown -R prometheus:prometheus /etc/prometheus/consoles
sudo chown -R prometheus:prometheus /etc/prometheus/console_libraries
Subtask 1.3: Create Prometheus Configuration
Create the main configuration file for Prometheus:

sudo nano /etc/prometheus/prometheus.yml
Add the following configuration:

global:
  scrape_interval: 15s
  evaluation_interval: 15s

rule_files:
  # - "first_rules.yml"
  # - "second_rules.yml"

scrape_configs:
  - job_name: 'prometheus'
    static_configs:
      - targets: ['localhost:9090']
  
  - job_name: 'node_exporter'
    static_configs:
      - targets: ['localhost:9100']
Set proper ownership:

sudo chown prometheus:prometheus /etc/prometheus/prometheus.yml
Subtask 1.4: Create Prometheus Service
Create a systemd service file:

sudo nano /etc/systemd/system/prometheus.service
Add the following content:

[Unit]
Description=Prometheus
Wants=network-online.target
After=network-online.target

[Service]
User=prometheus
Group=prometheus
Type=simple
ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/prometheus \
    --config.file /etc/prometheus/prometheus.yml \
    --storage.tsdb.path /var/lib/prometheus/ \
    --web.console.templates=/etc/prometheus/consoles \
    --web.console.libraries=/etc/prometheus/console_libraries \
    --web.listen-address=0.0.0.0:9090 \
    --web.enable-lifecycle

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
Subtask 1.5: Start Prometheus Service
# Reload systemd and start Prometheus
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl enable prometheus
sudo systemctl start prometheus

# Check service status
sudo systemctl status prometheus

# Verify Prometheus is running
curl http://localhost:9090/metrics
Task 2: Install and Configure InfluxDB
Subtask 2.1: Install InfluxDB
Add the InfluxDB repository and install:

# Add InfluxDB repository key
wget -qO- https://repos.influxdata.com/influxdb.key | sudo apt-key add -

# Add repository
echo "deb https://repos.influxdata.com/ubuntu $(lsb_release -cs) stable" | sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/influxdb.list

# Update package list
sudo apt update

# Install InfluxDB
sudo apt install influxdb -y
Subtask 2.2: Configure InfluxDB
Edit the InfluxDB configuration file:

sudo nano /etc/influxdb/influxdb.conf
Find and modify these sections (uncomment and set values):

[http]
  enabled = true
  bind-address = ":8086"
  auth-enabled = false
  log-enabled = true
  write-tracing = false
  pprof-enabled = true
  debug-pprof-enabled = false
  https-enabled = false
Subtask 2.3: Start InfluxDB Service
# Enable and start InfluxDB
sudo systemctl enable influxdb
sudo systemctl start influxdb

# Check service status
sudo systemctl status influxdb

# Verify InfluxDB is running
curl -i http://localhost:8086/ping
Subtask 2.4: Create Sample Database and Data
# Access InfluxDB CLI
influx

# Create a database
CREATE DATABASE grafana_lab

# Use the database
USE grafana_lab

# Insert sample data
INSERT cpu_usage,host=server1,region=us-west value=80.5
INSERT cpu_usage,host=server2,region=us-east value=65.2
INSERT memory_usage,host=server1,region=us-west value=75.8
INSERT memory_usage,host=server2,region=us-east value=82.1

# Verify data insertion
SELECT * FROM cpu_usage
SELECT * FROM memory_usage

# Exit InfluxDB CLI
exit
Task 3: Install and Configure Grafana
Subtask 3.1: Install Grafana
# Add Grafana repository
sudo apt-get install -y software-properties-common
wget -q -O - https://packages.grafana.com/gpg.key | sudo apt-key add -
echo "deb https://packages.grafana.com/oss/deb stable main" | sudo tee -a /etc/apt/sources.list.d/grafana.list

# Update and install Grafana
sudo apt-get update
sudo apt-get install grafana -y

# Enable and start Grafana
sudo systemctl enable grafana-server
sudo systemctl start grafana-server

# Check service status
sudo systemctl status grafana-server
Subtask 3.2: Access Grafana Web Interface
# Verify Grafana is accessible
curl http://localhost:3000

# Get your machine's IP address for browser access
ip addr show | grep inet
Open your web browser and navigate to http://localhost:3000 (or your machine's IP address with port 3000).

Default login credentials:

Username: admin
Password: admin
You will be prompted to change the password on first login.

Task 4: Connect Grafana to Prometheus
Subtask 4.1: Add Prometheus Data Source
In Grafana web interface, click on Configuration (gear icon) in the left sidebar
Select Data Sources
Click Add data source
Select Prometheus from the list
Subtask 4.2: Configure Prometheus Connection
Fill in the following details:

Name: Prometheus
URL: http://localhost:9090
Access: Server (default)
HTTP Method: GET
Leave other settings as default.

Subtask 4.3: Test Prometheus Connection
Scroll down and click Save & Test
You should see a green message: "Data source is working"
If the test fails, verify:

# Check if Prometheus is running
sudo systemctl status prometheus

# Check if port 9090 is listening
sudo netstat -tlnp | grep 9090

# Test direct access
curl http://localhost:9090/api/v1/query?query=up
Task 5: Connect Grafana to InfluxDB
Subtask 5.1: Add InfluxDB Data Source
In Grafana, go to Configuration > Data Sources
Click Add data source
Select InfluxDB from the list
Subtask 5.2: Configure InfluxDB Connection
Fill in the following details:

Name: InfluxDB
URL: http://localhost:8086
Access: Server (default)
Database: grafana_lab
User: (leave empty for this lab)
Password: (leave empty for this lab)
Subtask 5.3: Test InfluxDB Connection
Click Save & Test
You should see a green message: "Data source is working"
If the test fails, verify:

# Check if InfluxDB is running
sudo systemctl status influxdb

# Check if port 8086 is listening
sudo netstat -tlnp | grep 8086

# Test direct access
curl -i http://localhost:8086/ping

# Verify database exists
influx -execute "SHOW DATABASES"
Task 6: Test Data Source Connections
Subtask 6.1: Create Test Dashboard for Prometheus
Click + (plus icon) in the left sidebar
Select Dashboard
Click Add new panel
In the Query section:
Select Prometheus as data source
Enter query: up
Click Apply
Save the dashboard as "Test Dashboard"
Subtask 6.2: Add InfluxDB Panel
In the same dashboard, click Add panel
Select InfluxDB as data source
In the query builder:
FROM: cpu_usage
SELECT: field(value)
GROUP BY: tag(host)
Click Apply
Subtask 6.3: Verify Data Visualization
You should now see:

A panel showing Prometheus "up" metrics
A panel showing CPU usage data from InfluxDB
Subtask 6.4: Test Query Functionality
Test some additional queries:

For Prometheus:

# Check Prometheus targets
prometheus_notifications_total

# Check scrape duration
prometheus_target_scrape_duration_seconds
For InfluxDB:

SELECT mean("value") FROM "cpu_usage" WHERE time >= now() - 1h GROUP BY time(5m), "host"
Troubleshooting Common Issues
Prometheus Connection Issues
# Check Prometheus logs
sudo journalctl -u prometheus -f

# Verify configuration syntax
sudo /usr/local/bin/promtool check config /etc/prometheus/prometheus.yml

# Check firewall settings
sudo ufw status
InfluxDB Connection Issues
# Check InfluxDB logs
sudo journalctl -u influxdb -f

# Verify InfluxDB is responding
curl -sl -I http://localhost:8086/ping

# Check database permissions
influx -execute "SHOW USERS"
Grafana Connection Issues
# Check Grafana logs
sudo journalctl -u grafana-server -f

# Verify Grafana configuration
sudo cat /etc/grafana/grafana.ini | grep -v "^#" | grep -v "^$"

# Check port availability
sudo netstat -tlnp | grep 3000
Verification Steps
Step 1: Verify All Services Are Running
# Check all services
sudo systemctl status prometheus grafana-server influxdb

# Check all ports are listening
sudo netstat -tlnp | grep -E "(3000|8086|9090)"
Step 2: Verify Data Sources in Grafana
Go to Configuration > Data Sources
Confirm both Prometheus and InfluxDB show green status
Test each connection individually
Step 3: Create Sample Queries
Test these queries in Grafana's Explore section:

Prometheus queries:

up
prometheus_config_last_reload_successful
InfluxDB queries:

SELECT * FROM cpu_usage
SELECT mean(value) FROM memory_usage GROUP BY host
